Before the Arduino can talk, you must initialize the Serial port in `setup()`.

```cpp
void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
}
```

The `9600` is the **baud rate** — the speed at which they talk (9600 bits per second). Both the Arduino and the Serial Monitor window must be set to the exact same baud rate, or the message will look like garbage text!

Once initialized, use `Serial.println()` inside `loop()` to send text.

```cpp
void loop() {
  Serial.println("Hello Monster Lab!");
  delay(1000);
}
```

This prints the text and moves to a new line (that's what the `ln` in `println` means). It's great for debugging! If your robot isn't moving, you can print a sensor's value to see what the Arduino is actually reading.
